 "signs": [
  {
   "x": 40,
   "y": 66,
   "text": "NEPTUNE'S STAIRCASE. Eight locks in a row, a staircase for boats, lifting them about 20 metres. Thomas Telford built it for the Caledonian Canal, opened in 1822. The boats wait. The swans don't."
  },
  {
   "x": 23,
   "y": 36,
   "text": "THE GREAT GLEN. A scar across Scotland about 100 km long, from Fort William to Inverness, with a chain of lochs along its floor. The Great Glen Way walks the lot. Take sandwiches."
  },
  {
   "x": 21,
   "y": 20,
   "text": "AN GLEANN MÒR. The Gaelic name of the Great Glen, 'the big glen', as plain a name as you'll find. Plain names are the best ones. Nobody ever lost a loch because the sign was too clear."
  },
  {
   "x": 26,
   "y": 62,
   "plaque": "great_glen_invergarry",
   "title": "Invergarry Castle",
   "text": "Invergarry Castle, seat of the MacDonells of Glengarry, stands on Raven's Rock above Loch Oich. Bonnie Prince Charlie stopped here on his way south in 1745, and again as a fugitive after Culloden. Government troops burned it soon afterwards. The ravens, at least, still call."
  },
  {
   "x": 25,
   "y": 35,
   "plaque": "great_glen_fault",
   "title": "The Great Glen Fault",
   "text": "The glen follows a great crack in the Earth's crust, the Great Glen Fault. Around 400 million years ago the land to the north-west slid sideways along it by tens of kilometres at least, and ice later scoured the line into a chain of lochs. Small earthquakes still rattle it now and then."
  }
 ],
